- Story & Context: In the humid isolation of Hydroponics Bay 4, communications clerk Clara Viterbo sat under the sickly yellow glow of grow-lamps, clutching a decoded parchment dispatch that had trailed the vessel through decades of transit lag. The letter, penned by a Buenos Aires port worker, contained a marriage proposal meant for Clara’s grandmother, whose frozen remains had already been jettisoned into deep space thirty-two years prior. Swallowing the bitter taste of cold chicory broth, Clara traded her weekly calorie ration to bribe the audio technician, laying down a sobbing accordion counterpoint over a recorded message that ended with the hushed, devastating refrain, "You promised to wait by the river, but I was born between the cold stars." Her spoken testimony transformed private family grief into an anthem for an entire generation doomed to inherit someone else’s homesickness.
