Track 3: Tanda Sin Destino
- Story & Context: By the time the deceleration thrusters began their deafening six-month shudder toward the red glare of Proxima Centauri, the ritual of the dead letter had hardened into sacred folklore across the ship's habitat rings. At Station Terminus, old Mateo Cruz dragged his upright bass into the central commons alongside three accordionists, striking up a relentlessly dragging 2/4 rhythm while crew members cast thousands of late letters into a sputtering kerosene brazier, filling the air with the stinging aroma of burning linen and scorched glue. Nobody spoke during the six-hour milonga, but when the glowing paper embers began to float upward in the low gravity, a ragged choir shouted out the final line over the bellows’ wheeze: "Keep your new soil; we buried our hearts in the dead letter office." Decades later, planetary colonists still play this recording on landing anniversaries to remember the ancestral city they were never permitted to see.
